---
import Layout from '../../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import data from '../../../../data/topics.json';
import kidData from '../../../../data/kid-summaries.json';

const B = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/+$/, '') || '';
const { subjects, topics } = data;
const topic = topics.find((t) => t.slug === 'subjects/geography/foundational/latitude-and-climate');
const kidSummary = kidData[topic.slug] ?? null;
const subject = subjects.find((s) => s.id === topic.subject);
const seq = topics.filter((t) => t.status !== 'planned');
const idx = seq.indexOf(topic);
const prev = seq[(idx - 1 + seq.length) % seq.length];
const next = seq[(idx + 1) % seq.length];

const insolationCfg = {
  curves: [{ expr: '(1361/pi)*cos(x*pi/180)', label: 'sunlight at equinox', color: '#0f766e', width: 2.4 }],
  x: { min: -90, max: 90, label: 'latitude (°)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 470, label: 'W/m²' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 350,
};

const seasonsCfg = {
  preset: 'seasons',
  params: [
    { name: 'tilt', label: 'axial tilt', min: 0, max: 30, step: 0.5, value: 23.4, unit: '°' },
    { name: 'latitude', label: 'your latitude', min: -90, max: 90, step: 1, value: 40, unit: '°' },
  ],
  height: 340,
};

const citiesCfg = {
  kind: 'bar',
  xLabels: ['Singapore 1°N', 'Delhi 29°N', 'Cairo 30°N', 'Lima 12°S', 'Quito 0°', 'New York 41°N', 'London 52°N', 'Moscow 56°N', 'Reykjavík 64°N'],
  series: [{ label: 'annual mean temperature (°C)', values: [27.6, 25.3, 21.7, 19.0, 15.0, 13.1, 11.3, 5.8, 4.6] }],
  unit: '°C',
  digits: 1,
  height: 320,
};
---
<Layout title={`${topic.title} — ${subject.name} — Interactive Atlas`} description={topic.summary}>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="topic-grid">
      <aside class="topic-aside">
        <a class="back" href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>← all of {subject.name}</a>
        <dl>
          <dt>subject</dt><dd><span class="dot" style={`--dot:${subject.ink}; display:inline-block; margin-right:.4rem`}></span>{subject.name}</dd>
          <dt>level</dt><dd>{topic.level}</dd>
          <dt>time</dt><dd>{topic.minutes} min</dd>
          <dt>tags</dt><dd>{topic.tags.slice(0, 3).join(', ')}</dd>
        </dl>
      </aside>
      <article>
        <header class="topic-head">
          <p class="crumb"><a href={B + '/'}>atlas</a><span class="sep">/</span><a href={`${B}/subjects/${subject.id}/`}>{subject.name}</a><span class="sep">/</span>{topic.level}</p>
          <h1>{topic.title}</h1>
          <p class="lede">{topic.summary}</p>
        </header>

        {kidSummary && (
          <aside class="kid-note" aria-label="Summary for young readers">
            <p><strong>For young explorers:</strong> {kidSummary}</p>
          </aside>
        )}
        <div class="topic-body">
          <h2>The idea</h2>
          <p>
            First, two words people use interchangeably but shouldn't. <strong>Weather</strong> is
            what the atmosphere does today; <strong>climate</strong> is the average of weather over
            about 30 years, plus its moods — how variable, how extreme, how seasonal. London and
            Beijing have similar climates' averages in places; their weather behaves utterly
            differently. Climate is the stage; weather is the improvisation on it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Climate itself is mostly a geometry story. Because Earth is a sphere, sunlight arrives
            as parallel rays, and the same square metre of beam spreads over more and more ground
            as you move from the equator toward the poles — the classic torch-shining-on-a-ball
            effect. Beam spreading plus a longer path through the atmosphere means the
            <strong>tropics receive roughly twice the solar power of the poles</strong>: on the
            equinox model below, the equator averages about 433 watts per square metre while the
            poles get essentially nothing. Almost every climate map ever drawn — temperature,
            rainfall, vegetation, even where humans chose to farm — is downstream of that one
            geometric fact.
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 1</span><span>incoming sunlight by latitude at equinox — hover to read values</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="plotter" data-config={JSON.stringify(insolationCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <h2>Latitude and the sun's angle</h2>
          <p>
            The geometry is precise enough to calculate. The sun stands directly overhead only
            between the <strong>tropics</strong>, the latitudes 23.4° north and south (the
            Tropic of Cancer and Capricorn — the numbers mirror Earth's tilt). Outside that band
            the noon sun is always somewhat sideways, and the further you are from the overhead
            point, the more slanted and spread the light. At London (51.5°N) at equinox the noon
            sun is only 38.5° above the horizon — you can test the arithmetic with the rule
            <em>noon sun angle = 90° − |your latitude − the sun's declination|</em>. The
            consequences compound: slantwise light passes through more atmosphere (more of it
            absorbed and scattered), lights less ground per watt, and — above the <strong>Arctic
            and Antarctic Circles</strong> at 66.5° — disappears entirely for part of the year,
            the land of the midnight sun and the polar night. The equator's other privilege is
            consistency: it gets twelve-hour days and high sun all year, which is why tropical
            climates are about rain and seasonality, not temperature.
          </p>

          <h2>Then tilt breaks the symmetry</h2>
          <p>
            If Earth spun perfectly upright, every place would have eternal equinox weather and
            latitude alone would decide climate. But the axis leans by 23.4°, so through the year
            each hemisphere takes turns leaning into the light: June solstice (Northern summer)
            tips the north sunward, December solstice the south. Seasons are thus a
            <em>tilt</em> phenomenon, not a distance phenomenon — Earth is actually
            <strong>closest to the Sun in early January</strong>, the depth of northern winter.
            Tilt does two things at once: it changes how high the sun climbs (beam intensity) and
            how long the day lasts (hours of heating), and the two stack. Set the tilt to zero in
            the model below and seasons vanish everywhere; exaggerate it and even London gets
            monsoon-grade swings. Pick a latitude and watch its daylight loop:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 2</span><span>the seasons machine — drag the tilt and your latitude</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="sliderLab" data-config={JSON.stringify(seasonsCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The hemispheres mirror each other (our June is Australia's December), and the
            extremes bracket the scale: Mercury's axis barely tilts (0.03°) so it has almost no
            seasons, while Uranus is tipped 98° — rolling around its orbit — giving each pole 42
            years of daylight followed by 42 of dark. Earth's 23.4° sits in the Goldilocks
            middle: strong enough to drive a yearly rhythm that ecosystems and agriculture are
            built around, mild enough to keep most of the planet habitable.
          </p>

          <h2>From sunlight to circulation: the Hadley engine</h2>
          <p>
            Here is the step most people miss: sunlight does not stay where it lands. The tropics
            run a permanent energy surplus and the poles a permanent deficit, and fluids move
            heat. Air heated at the equator rises (carrying enormous evaporated moisture with
            it), cools, and dumps its rain — creating the thunderstorm belt of the
            <strong>Intertropical Convergence Zone</strong> and, on the ground beneath it, the
            rainforests. That air then flows poleward aloft and sinks near latitude 30°, arriving
            hot, dry and pressing down: the subtropical high-pressure belts where the Sahara,
            Arabia, the Kalahari and the Australian outback sit. The equator rains, 30° bakes —
            the world's deserts line up along a <em>latitude</em>, not a longitude, because the
            atmospheric engine puts them there. Further cells (the mid-latitude Ferrel and polar
            cells) continue the handoff, and Earth's rotation bends all the surface winds into
            the trade winds and westerlies — which is why the westerlies drench western Europe's
            coasts and the trades water tropical east coasts. Mountains add the last twist:
            air forced over a range drops its rain on the windward side and arrives dry, giving
            the Atacama and Gobi their rain shadows.
          </p>

          <h2>Climate zones — and what bends them</h2>
          <p>
            Follow the insolation curve and the classical zones fall out: the <strong>tropics</strong>
            (sun overhead, rain-heavy, no winter), the <strong>temperate</strong> bands (sun
            slanted but never absent, four seasons), and the <strong>polar caps</strong> (long
            dayless winters). Köppen's classification, still the world standard, formalises
            these with rainfall and temperature data into the A–E zones on every atlas's back
            page. But latitude only sets the frame — the chart below shows real cities breaking
            it:
          </p>

          <figure class="widget">
            <figcaption><span class="fig-no">fig. 3</span><span>annual mean temperature against latitude — find the two rule-breakers</span></figcaption>
            <div class="widget-body">
              <div data-widget="chart" data-config={JSON.stringify(citiesCfg)}></div>
            </div>
          </figure>

          <p>
            The rule holds in the broad pattern (each 10° of latitude costs a few degrees), but
            look at the exceptions, because each is a modifier wearing a costume. <strong>Quito
            sits on the equator</strong> yet averages around 15 °C: it stands at 2,850 m, and
            temperature falls about 6.5 °C per kilometre of altitude — mountains make their own
            latitude, which is why equatorial Kilimanjaro (5,895 m) carries snow. <strong>Lima
            is cold for 12°S</strong> because the Humboldt Current hauls icy water up its coast.
            And <strong>London is absurdly warm for 51.5°N</strong> — the Gulf Stream's ocean
            heat transport, worth several degrees, plus maritime moderation — while latitudinal
            twin cities in continental interiors (Irkutsk, at a similar latitude in Siberia,
            averages near 1 °C) show what the ocean's absence does. Add the <strong>monsoons</strong>
            — seasonal wind reversals driven by land heating faster than sea, which switch the
            Indian subcontinent between drenching summer and parched winter — and you have the
            complete toolkit: latitude sets the frame; altitude, ocean, currents and wind belts
            bend it.
          </p>

          <h2>Reading a climate graph: the geographer's first skill</h2>
  <p>
    Climate graphs pack an entire station's personality into one frame — bars for monthly
    rainfall, a line for monthly temperature — and reading four of them is a complete tour
    of this chapter. <strong>Singapore</strong> (1.4°N): the temperature line is flat near
    27 °C all year (no winter — the equator's consistency) and the bars are tall and
    near-uniform (rain every month, ITCZ overhead): the tropical rainforest climate.
    <strong>London</strong> (51.5°N): the line arcs from ~5 °C in January to ~18 °C in July
    (range ~13 °C — maritime moderation), bars moderate year-round with a slight autumn
    peak: the temperate maritime climate the westerlies water. <strong>Mumbai</strong>
    (19°N): temperature stays warm but the rainfall bars are a cliff — near-zero for eight
    months, then 700+ mm in July alone: the monsoon, the ITCZ arriving with its
    rain-band. <strong>Cairo</strong> (30°N): warm line, bars nearly empty — the
    subtropical desert under the Hadley cell's descending air. Every climate graph you will
    ever read is one of these four archetypes or a blend, and the diagnostic questions are
    always the same: what is the temperature <em>range</em> (continentality), when does the
    rain fall (pressure belts), and how <em>much</em> in the wettest month (how hard the
    engine is running overhead)?
  </p>

  <h2>El Niño: the climate's irregular heartbeat</h2>
  <p>
    One complication deserves full section status, because it reorganises the global map
    every few years. Under normal conditions, Pacific trade winds blow east-to-west, piling
    warm water into a vast pool near Indonesia; cold, nutrient-rich water wells up off Peru
    (the Humboldt system that keeps Lima cool and its anchovy fishery the world's largest).
    Every two to seven years the trades slacken and the warm pool sloshes east —
    <strong>El Niño</strong> ("the Christ child", named by Peruvian fishermen for its
    Christmas arrival). Peru's cold upwelling fails (fisheries collapse), its coastal
    deserts flood, and the consequences broadcast worldwide: Australia and Indonesia tip
    into drought and fire, the Indian monsoon weakens, Atlantic hurricanes are suppressed,
    and the globe's average temperature steps up a notch — the record years of 1998 and
    2016 and 2023–24 were all El Niño years, which is why climate scientists carefully
    separate its wobble from the underlying warming trend. Its mirror-image sister,
    <strong>La Niña</strong> (trades stronger, warm pool shoved further west) roughly
    reverses the pattern. The lesson for the chapter: latitude sets the stage, but the
    ocean — with a thousand times the atmosphere's heat capacity — periodically rewrites
    the script, and the two systems are one coupled machine.
  </p>

  <h2>The record book: climate's extremes</h2>
  <p>
    The extreme end of every climate dial makes the mechanism vivid. The highest air
    temperature reliably recorded is 56.7 °C at Death Valley (1913) — a below-sea-level
    desert basin where descending air compresses and heats, the Hadley engine's downdraught
    at full power. The lowest, −89.2 °C, came from Vostok station on the Antarctic plateau
    (1983) — the pole's permanent energy deficit plus 3,400 m of ice-sheet altitude, the two
    cold factors stacked. Mawsynram in the Indian hills averages around 11,800 mm of rain a
    year as monsoon air is forced up the Khasi Hills — orographic rainfall at championship
    scale — while parts of Chile's Atacama Desert have weather stations that have recorded
    <em>no</em> measurable rain for years: cold ocean current, subtropical high, and a
    rain-shadowing mountain range, all three drought mechanisms in one place. And the
    windiest sea-level place on Earth, Cape Denison in Antarctica (annual mean near
    70–80 km/h), is the katabatic engine's showpiece. Each record is a textbook process
    turned up to maximum — which is why examiners love asking not just "where?" but "why
    there?"
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
          <p>
            The global energy budget puts numbers on the engine. Of the ~340 W/m² arriving at
            the top of the atmosphere (averaged over the whole sphere and year), about 30%
            reflects straight back (clouds, ice, atmosphere); the rest is absorbed, and the
            tropics' surplus versus the poles' deficit is cancelled by a poleward heat
            transport of around 5 petawatts — carried by the atmosphere and, importantly, by
            ocean currents, which alone move about a quarter. The Hadley cell is not fixed,
            either: it expands and contracts with the seasons (dragging the monsoon rains north
            and south over India), and climate models project it widening poleward in a warming
            century — pushing the desert belts into the Mediterranean and the American
            Southwest's margins. On geological timescales the geometry itself wobbles:
            Milankovitch cycles — slow changes in orbit shape (~100,000 yr), tilt (41,000 yr,
            between 22.1° and 24.5°) and equinox precession (26,000 yr) — repack the insolation
            and pace the ice ages; the last glacial maximum, 20,000 years ago, had ice sheets
            kilometres thick over Chicago and London. Climate is geometry with a memory — and
            the memory is written in ice cores and seafloor mud.
          </p>

          <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> NOAA Solar Calculator and global station data; WMO definition of climate normals; Köppen climate classification (1884/1936); Hartmann, <em>Global Physical Climatology</em>.</p>
        </div>

        <nav class="topic-next" aria-label="Lesson navigation">
          <div><span class="tn-label">previous lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${prev.slug}/`}>{prev.title}</a></div>
          <div style="text-align:right"><span class="tn-label">next lesson</span><a href={`${B}/${next.slug}/`}>{next.title}</a></div>
        </nav>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
